    bio: 'Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling was born in Leonberg, Württemberg, in 1775, the son of a Lutheran clergyman and scholar. A child prodigy of staggering precocity, he entered the Tübingen Stift theological seminary at the age of fifteen, where he shared rooms with the poet Friedrich Hölderlin and the philosopher G.W.F. Hegel, forming one of the most intellectually explosive friendships in the history of philosophy. By his early twenties he had already published major works on the philosophy of nature and had been appointed professor at Jena, the intellectual epicenter of German Romanticism, where he became intimately connected with the circle around the Schlegel brothers, Novalis, and Schiller. His early brilliance was of a kind that made it seem, for a time, as if the future of German philosophy belonged entirely to him.\n\nSchelling\'s philosophical development was restless and many-phased, a quality that made him frustrating to his contemporaries and endlessly fascinating to later interpreters. His Naturphilosophie argued that nature was not dead mechanism but living spirit in its unconscious form — a self-organizing, self-differentiating whole in which polarity and dynamic process were fundamental. His Philosophy of Identity proposed that the Absolute — the ultimate ground of reality — was the identity of nature and spirit, subject and object, prior to any distinction between them. His later Freedom essay of 1809 introduced the concept of evil into idealist metaphysics with a seriousness and depth that shocked his contemporaries: freedom, Schelling argued, is real only because it includes the genuine possibility of choosing against the good, of willing the ground of existence against its own higher destiny. This was not a position his contemporaries, including Hegel, had been prepared to accommodate.\n\nIn his final decades, Schelling developed what he called his "positive philosophy" — a turn against the entire rationalist tradition, including his own earlier systematic idealism, toward a philosophy grounded in the sheer facticity of existence rather than its logical necessity. Existence, he argued, is prior to essence: the that of the world is not deducible from any what. This move, which he developed in his Berlin lectures after 1841 — lectures attended by Søren Kierkegaard, Friedrich Engels, and Mikhail Bakunin — anticipates existentialism by a century and represents perhaps the most underappreciated chapter in the history of European philosophy. He died in Bad Ragaz, Switzerland, in 1854, having outlived his philosophical reputation by decades, his later work largely ignored until the twentieth century began to rediscover its extraordinary depths.',
